# FLOOR 8: THE MERCHANT'S CROSSING
*"Five days. One stall. The market doesn't care who you are — only what you're selling."*

*This is the complete, merged version of Floor 8 — all economy, robbery, reputation, and Syndicate-interruption revisions are integrated directly into the text below. There is no separate addendum; this document is the floor.*

---

## LORE: VERATH'S CROSSING

Three roads meet at Verath's Crossing. They call them the Silver Road, the Dustway, and the High Path — and for two centuries, the town that grew at their junction was one of the most prosperous in any land. Goods moved through Verath's Crossing the way water moves through a channel: constantly, naturally, with no one owning the flow.

That changed when the **Iron Ledger** arrived.

No one remembers exactly when the syndicate took hold. It happened the way most corruption does — gradually, then all at once. A tax here. A protection fee there. A word in the right ear that made a competitor's shipment arrive damaged. Within a generation, the Iron Ledger controlled every major supply line in the town. Independent merchants who refused to pay tribute found their goods spoiled, their customers warned away, their reputations dismantled by whispers.

**Vael Geldrun** was the town's Trade Arbitrator — a neutral judge who resolved commercial disputes fairly and without favor. The Iron Ledger bought him with patience. First a favor. Then a debt. Then an offer he couldn't refuse, made in a room with no witnesses. By the time anyone noticed what he had become, it was too late.

His power came from a pact with **The Hollow Ledger** — a primordial entity that exists in the space between transactions. It is not a god. It is what greed becomes when it outlives the people who felt it. Centuries of broken contracts and unfulfilled promises and stolen coins, compressed into a will.

The Hollow Ledger gave Vael the ability to sense all transactions within the town, shift perceived value with his attention, and make debt feel like physics. Merchants paid — not because the Iron Ledger scared them, though it did, but because Vael's presence made the alternative feel impossible.

When the Tower consumed this place, the corruption froze it. The town became a Floor: a snapshot of the moment Verath's Crossing was at its most strangled. Merchants still set up their stalls. Customers still come to buy. But the Iron Ledger's network runs underneath everything, invisible and hungry, and Vael watches from the shadow of his arbitrator's tower at the market's edge.

The floor's logic is simple: commerce happened here once. It can happen again. The question is who does it better.

