## ENEMY ENCOUNTERS — THE SYNDICATE

The Iron Ledger's network runs underneath the floor. Its agents operate through economic pressure, sabotage, and manipulation — not open violence inside the bazaar. Outside the bazaar, the rules change entirely.

### Syndicate Pressure Level

The DM tracks a hidden **Syndicate Pressure** value from 1 to 10. It starts at **4**.

| Level | State |
|---|---|
| 1–3 | Quiet. Agents are cautious. Fewer random events. |
| 4–6 | Active. Standard enemy frequency. |
| 7–9 | Aggressive. Enemies appear more often. Arsonist becomes possible. |
| 10 | Crisis. All enemy types active simultaneously. Grey Ledger has unlimited stock. |

**Pressure Increases When:** any stall leads all others by 20gp+ (+1) · a Syndicate agent is driven off without being publicly exposed (+1) · a Tax Collector is completely ignored (+2) · players miss the Underground on Day 3 or later by choice (+1)

**Pressure Decreases When:** a Syndicate agent is publicly exposed (−1) · a Syndicate agent is defeated outside the bazaar (−2) · players donate goods to village residents (−1) · the Iron Ledger Charter is found (−3, immediate)

### SELLING PHASE INTERRUPTIONS — the harassment, scheduled

This is how the Syndicate actually shows up: periodically, **a customer slot becomes a Syndicate event instead of a sale.** It eats the exact thing it's meant to eat — time the player could have spent selling — because that customer simply doesn't happen; the agent does.

For every customer slot in a stall's queue, before resolving the normal Customer Table roll, roll **1d20** modified by current Syndicate Pressure:

| Syndicate Pressure | Interruption Chance |
|---|---|
| 1–3 | Roll 1–2 on d20 (10%) |
| 4–6 | Roll 1–3 on d20 (15%) |
| 7–9 | Roll 1–4 on d20 (20%) |
| 10 | Roll 1–6 on d20 (30%) |

At the starting Pressure of 4, that's roughly **1 interruption per stall per day** on average — and it climbs fast as Pressure rises, which is the intended shape: a quiet Day 1, a genuinely harassed Day 4–5 if the party hasn't been pushing Pressure back down.

On an interruption, roll **1d8**:

| d8 | Result |
|---|---|
| 1 | **Tax Collector arrives** — always resolves against the *current leading stall*, even if a different stall's queue triggered the roll. If you're not the leader, narrate him passing your line on his way to theirs; your slot is still consumed. Use the Tax Collector encounter table below. |
| 2 | **Slanderer** spotted working the crowd near your stall. *Day 3+ only — reroll if earlier.* Use the Slanderer encounter table below. |
| 3 | **Cutpurse Ring** sweeps through. *Day 2+ only — reroll if earlier.* Use the Cutpurse Ring encounter table below. |
| 4 | A customer repeats a **Counterfeit Ring** rumor about your goods. *Only if triggered — some stall has sold a single item for 15gp+ — reroll if not yet active.* Use the Counterfeit Ring encounter table below. |
| 5 | **Mark-Hunter** reaches the front of your line. *Day 2+ only — reroll if earlier.* Use the Mark-Hunter encounter table below. (This replaces the old standalone "roll 1d20 alongside every customer" trigger — Mark-Hunters now only appear through this unified roll.) |
| 6 | One of Edrin Voss's "shoppers" quietly compares your prices to the **Grey Ledger's**. *Day 3+ only, Grey Ledger must be open — reroll if not.* No check required: DM notes your current best-selling category; the Grey Ledger stocks and undercuts it tomorrow. |
| 7 | **False alarm.** A tense moment — nothing happens. The slot is still lost; the scare itself was the cost. |
| 8 | **DM's call** — pick whichever active threat best fits the current Day and Pressure level. |

If a reroll lands on another unavailable entry, default to **7 (False Alarm)** rather than looping.

**The Arsonist is not part of this table** — it operates separately, late in the day or overnight (see below), targeting the leading stall directly rather than consuming a customer slot.

